igures":[{"name":"Kai Bird","confidence":0.99}],"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Jerusalem","confidence":0.98},{"name":"Israel","confidence":0.9}],"argument":{"present":true,"statement":"Growing up amid divided cities and regional wars gave Kai Bird a firsthand perspective on the roots and widening of the Arab-Israeli conflict.","confidence":0.86},"academicOrientation":{"score":28,"confidence":0.94},"profileConfidence":0.94,"lowerConfidenceCandidates":{"centralPlaces":[{"name":"Lebanon","confidence":0.7}]},"model":"gpt-5.6-luna","promptVersion":8,"inputHash":"804f8d9f76498216e6e9fdd04cf28bfd887f7febe9bd50988540adc04693069d","reviewStatus":"flagged","validationWarnings":["author_included_for_review: Kai Bird"]},"primarySubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","secondarySubjects":["Politics & Government","War & Military"],"confidence":"high","rationale":"The book primarily functions as a memoir recounting the author's personal experiences growing up during turbulent times in the Middle East while also addressing political and military conflicts.","method":"llm","model":"gpt-4o-mini","reviewStatus":"generated","inputHash":"427530d4446a40a70129c9073fe46a3883c8b57b8bfa68b7ea33d39e726ea3e7","summary":"PULITZER PRIZE WINNER KAI BIRD’S fascinating memoir of his early years spent in Israel, Jordan, Egypt, Saudi Arabia, and Lebanon provides an original and illuminating perspective into the Arab-Israeli conflict. Weeks before the Suez War of 1956, four-year-old Kai Bird, son of a garrulous, charming American Foreign Service officer, moved to Jerusalem with his family. They settled in a small house, where young Kai could hear church bells and the Muslim call to prayer and watch as donkeys and camels competed with cars for space on the narrow streets. Each day on his way to school, Kai was driven through Mandelbaum Gate, where armed soldiers guarded the line separating Israeli-controlled West Jerusalem from Arab-controlled East. He had a front-seat view to both sides of a divided city—and the roots of the widening conflict between Arabs and Israelis. Bird would spend much of his life crossing such lines—as a child in Jerusalem, Saudi Arabia, and Egypt, and later, as a young man in Lebanon. Crossing Mandelbaum Gate is his compelling personal history of growing up an American in the midst of three major wars and three turbulent decades in the Middle East. The Zelig-like Bird brings readers into such conflicts as the Suez War, the Six Day War of 1967, and the Black September hijackings in 1970 that triggered the Jordanian civil war.","pageCount":448,"publicationYear":2010,"subjectCategories":[{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","label":"Biography & Autobiography","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-crossing-mandelbaum-gate-coming-of-age-between-the-arabs-and-israelis-1956-1978-kai-bird"}],"displaySummary":"The Zelig-like Bird brings readers into such conflicts as the Suez War, the Six Day War of 1967, and the Black September hijackings in 1970 that triggered the Jordanian civil war.","primaryTopic":"Intellectual Biography","subjectEvidence":{"primarySubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","confidence":"high","method":"evidence_score","candidates":[{"subject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"evidenceCount":1}],"evidence":[{"source":"llm_classifier","rawLabel":"Memoir & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":8,"confidence":"high","note":"The book primarily functions as a memoir recounting the author's personal experiences growing up during turbulent times in the Middle East while also addressing political and military conflicts.","id":"subject-evidence-book-crossing-mandelbaum-gate-coming-of-age-between-the-arabs-and-israelis-1956-1978-kai-bird-1"},{"source":"google_books","scheme":"google_books_category","rawLabel":"Biography & Autobiography","mappedSubject":"Memoir & Autobiography","score":7,"confidence":"high","sourceId":"source-google-books-summary-crossing-mandelbaum-gate-coming-of-age-between-the-arabs-an